WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:04.840
Disney's live-action remake of one of their
most treasured animations, Beauty And The

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:08.650
Beast, must have been a formidable challenge
for the filmmakers as they had to decide how

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:13.719
to re-imagine iconic characters, scenes, songs,
and locations from the 1991 Oscar-winning

00:00:13.719 --> 00:00:14.719
movie.

00:00:14.719 --> 00:00:18.250
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and in my last video I revealed some incredible

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:22.671
facts you probably didn't know about Disney's
animated classic, but now, in this video,

00:00:22.671 --> 00:00:27.140
I want to let you in on some amazing secrets
and facts about the making of the new live-action

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:29.200
Beauty And The Beast.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:32.860
Costume designer Jacqueline Durran tested
lots of different shades of yellow on camera

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:35.530
before choosing the final colour for Belle's
ball gown.

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:42.160
Once that was decided, the dress took 180
feet of satin organza, 3,000 feet of thread,

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:46.819
over 2,000 Swarovski crystals, and more than
12,000 hours to make!

00:00:46.819 --> 00:00:51.079
Gold leaf filigree was also printed on the
top two layers of the gown in a design which

00:00:51.079 --> 00:00:53.280
matched the ballroom floor.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:57.239
As for the dress Belle wears right at the
end of the movie, the print for that was actually

00:00:57.239 --> 00:01:01.999
based on an original 18th-century apron that
the film's costume designer bought during

00:01:01.999 --> 00:01:03.609
her student days.

00:01:03.609 --> 00:01:08.230
The household objects like Lumiere, Cogsworth,
and Mrs Potts were brought to life using a

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:13.320
time-consuming mix of computer-generated effects
in post-production and solid models during

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:15.010
on-set filming.

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:18.430
For example, a real version of the wardrobe
Garderobe was built and rigged with pulleys

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:20.789
and various devices so she could move on set.

00:01:20.789 --> 00:01:24.750
But, when we see Garderobe dress Belle, that
was created thanks to the magic of visual

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:27.140
effects in post-production.

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:31.939
As for Lumiere, the filmmakers wanted to incorporate
actor Ewan McGregor's personality into the

00:01:31.939 --> 00:01:36.620
singing candelabra, so they used performance
capture technology to film McGregor moving

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:39.010
and dancing how he thought Lumiere would.

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:43.420
Also, before filming began, McGregor and the
other actors who played the enchanted characters

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:47.170
recorded their dialogue so it could be used
as a guide for the cast and crew during the

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:48.299
actual shoot.

00:01:48.299 --> 00:01:52.829
So, when Dan Stevens was filming a scene with
Lumiere, for instance, he was acting opposite

00:01:52.829 --> 00:01:57.380
an LED light on a stick and hearing McGregor's
pre-recorded voice!

00:01:57.380 --> 00:02:01.670
Then during post-production, McGregor and
his fellow enchanted character actors recorded

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:05.479
their dialogue again so it matched the tone
of what was happening on screen.

00:02:05.479 --> 00:02:09.950
Apparently, the most difficult object to design,
according to director Bill Condon, was Mrs

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:10.950
Potts.

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:15.260
Although the animated version of the character
has a spout for a nose, Condon told the Hollywood

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:19.300
Reporter that when they tried that for the
live-action version, she ended up looking

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:23.260
'like a pig', and he added that 'there was
no way to make that spout nose appealing in

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:25.099
three dimensions.'

00:02:25.099 --> 00:02:29.550
When you see the servants in their human form,
do look out for how their costumes and hairstyles

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:32.690
mirror their appearance as enchanted objects!

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:36.629
For example, the buttons on Cogsworth's human
costume have Roman numerals on them, just

00:02:36.629 --> 00:02:37.970
like his clock-face.

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:41.720
And the design on Cogsworth's waistcoat and
epaulettes also echoes the pattern we see

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:43.080
on him when he's a clock.

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:47.340
And if you look at his hairstyle, you'll notice
that it's inspired by the shape of Cogsworth

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:48.990
the clock's head!

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:53.040
Bringing to life the Busby Berkeley-style
song-and-dance number, "Be Our Guest", was

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:58.129
a massive challenge that took the movie's
crew six months to prepare, one month to film,

00:02:58.129 --> 00:02:59.930
and more than twelve months to finish.

00:02:59.930 --> 00:03:05.080
When I spoke to director Bill Condon recently,
he said that initially they did consider creating

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:09.379
the Beast using prosthetic make-up, but because
that wouldn't have allowed a subtle facial

00:03:09.379 --> 00:03:12.970
performance to come through properly, they
didn't pursue that option too long before

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:17.659
deciding to go high-tech and combine physical
and facial performance capture to bring the

00:03:17.659 --> 00:03:18.750
Beast to life.

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:23.650
The physical performance capture part involved
Stevens acting alongside fellow cast members

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:28.370
on practical sets, which was anything but
easy as he had to wear 10-inch metal stilts

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:33.330
as well as a prosthetics muscle suit that
weighed 18kg and a grey Lycra bodysuit covered

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:35.330
in visual effects markers.

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:39.720
And beneath all that, Stevens also wore a
special cooling vest which the crew plugged

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:42.830
in between takes to help regulate his temperature.

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:47.079
The second part of playing the Beast took
place later and began with Dan Stevens' face

00:03:47.079 --> 00:03:51.510
being covered in phosphorescent make-up, which
looks blue in ultraviolet light.

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:55.690
With his special make-up on, Stevens then
acted out all his scenes again, but this time

00:03:55.690 --> 00:04:00.329
he did so from just the neck up, sitting alone
in a booth, surrounded by cameras to capture

00:04:00.329 --> 00:04:04.640
his facial performance, which the movie's
VFX teams then used to give life to the Beast's

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:06.860
face on the big screen.

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:11.469
Belle's hometown of Villeneuve was built on
the huge backlot at Shepperton Studios, and

00:04:11.469 --> 00:04:16.049
was the movie's biggest set, measuring over
28,000 square feet.

00:04:16.049 --> 00:04:19.810
And that size was certainly necessary for
the film's first musical number which features

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:25.460
over 150 extras, hundreds of animals, 28 wagons,
and a myriad of highly-detailed props and

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:27.120
set decorations.

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:30.900
When it came to designing the fictional town's
look, the movie's production designer took

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:34.490
inspiration from a village in the south of
France called Conques.

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:39.180
The film's story is set in mid-18th-century
France, so for the look of the Beast's castle,

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:43.460
the art department was heavily influenced
by the very ornate Rococo style popular during

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:49.139
that period and which included lots of intricate
designs, curved shapes, plant motifs, and

00:04:49.139 --> 00:04:50.139
gold.

00:04:50.139 --> 00:04:54.290
As for the castle's ballroom, that features
a 12,000 square foot faux-marble floor with

00:04:54.290 --> 00:04:58.530
a pattern that was actually inspired by the
ceiling design in a Benedictine monastery

00:04:58.530 --> 00:04:59.750
in Bavaria, Germany.

00:04:59.750 --> 00:05:04.210
And the ballroom's ten huge chandeliers are
based on ones from the Palace of Versailles

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:05.800
in France.

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:11.490
The set for the enchanted forest around the
castle features about 20,000 icicles, a frozen

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:15.720
lake, real trees, and a pair of 29-foot high
ice gates!

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:20.610
It was built on Shepperton Studios' biggest
stage, which measures over 9 and a half thousand

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:25.440
square feet, and it took the movie's crew
15 weeks to make it.

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:29.110
Before filming began, director Bill Condon
worked one-to-one with each of his actors

00:05:29.110 --> 00:05:32.080
to fine-tune their characters' personalities
and backstories.

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:36.480
After that, the cast did a table read of the
full script and they even performed some of

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:40.300
the musical numbers in full, giving the read-through
the feel of a live concert for the movie's

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:41.300
crew!

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:47.039
By the way, that musical read-through took
place on the 15 April 2015, which, coincidentally,

00:05:47.039 --> 00:05:52.199
was also the birthday of Emma Watson who plays
Belle, Luke Evans who plays Gaston, Emma Thompson

00:05:52.199 --> 00:05:55.289
who plays Mrs Potts, and Nathan Mack who plays
Chip.

